{"name":"Phoenix Flames","hex":"#faa21a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"phoenix","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mythological bird, said to be the only one of its kind, which lives for 500 years and then dies by burning to ashes on a pyre of its own making, ignited by the sun. It then arises anew from the ashes.","Anything that is reborn after apparently being destroyed."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To transfer assets from one company to another to dodge liability"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/phoenix"},{"term":"flames","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of flame"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of flame"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flames"}],"entries":[{"title":"Phoenix (mythology)","blocks":[{"text":"The phoenix is a legendary immortal bird that cyclically regenerates or is otherwise born again. Originating in Greek mythology, it has analogues in many cultures, such as Egyptian and Persian mythology. Associated with the Sun, a phoenix obtains new life by rising from the ashes of its predecessor. Some legends say it dies in a show of flames and combustion, while others say that it simply burns to death and decomposes before being born again. In the Motif-Index of Folk-Literature, a tool used by folklorists, the phoenix is classified as motif B32."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Phoenix_%281583%29.svg/330px-Phoenix_%281583%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phoenix_(mythology)","wikidata":"Q48444"},{"title":"Flame","blocks":[{"text":"A flame is the visible, gaseous part of a fire. It is caused by a highly exothermic chemical reaction made in a thin zone. When flames are hot enough to have ionized gaseous components of sufficient density, they are then considered plasma."}],"image":"https://thumb